speechtext:
Whereas great numbers of Canadian seamen on the Pacific coast of British Columbia are out of employment and in many cases destitute;
And whereas, owners of ships and vessels of Canadian registry on the Pacific coast of British Columbia give employment in large measure to seamen, cooks and engineers of oriental origin;
And whereas, other countries give greater consideration to the employment of their own nationals on ships and vessels belonging to their own country;
And whereas, the Canadian government grant large sums of money to the owners of ships and vessefls by way of subsidies;
And whereas, it is desirable that every encouragement be given for the employment of officers, seamen, cooks and engineers of white Canadian citizenship in preference to those of oriental origin on all Canadian owned and registered ships and vessels;
Therefore be it resolved, that, in the opinion of this house, the government take such steps as will provide for employment of greater numbers of white Canadian citizens on all ships and vessels of Canadian ownership and registry, the owners of which receive grants of money from the public treasury of Canada.
He said: Mr. Speaker, may I say at the outset that this is not the first time I have spoken on this matter in the house, but it is the first time that I have introduced it by way of a resolution. It is unnecessary to elaborate the meaning of the resolution as I think the wording sets out the gist of the matter fairly well.
I would like first to speak of the subsidies paid by the government of this country to the various steamships operating on the Pacific coast, and in passing to mention the total subsidies paid to all shipping by Canada. In the year 1930 the mail subsidies paid to ships operating on the Pacific amounted to $227,080. The figures I am giving are taken from the main estimates. In 1934 the subsidies paid to steamships on the Pacific had risen to almost a million dollars, to be exact, $990,800. In the estimates brought down the other day for 1935-36 the total for the Pacific ocean is $1,180,800. I ask the house to note the great increase in these estimates during

Pacific Coast Shipping
the past four or five years. I realize that a private company or an individual in business for himself is free to engage whomever he likes, and it might be going too far for this parliament or government to restrict that individual or company as to whom he should hire. But I claim that when this parliament and the country is granting to steamship companies such large sums, amounting in the aggregate to nearly two million dollars, we should take notice of whom these companies employ. I shall endeavour to prove that the lack of some supervision in this matter is working a severe hardship on our own white Canadian seamen.
As to the numbers employed on steamships operating on the Pacific may I give the latest figures? This information was provided on January 12, of this year. The figures show that orientals engaged numbered 2,590. The number of white Canadian seamen idle in the province of British Columbia, according to up to date figures, is roughly 2.050. A careful survey was made of the unemployment camps in order to secure the occupations of the men there employed, and it was discovered that some 400 of these men had been seamen. In the city of Victoria 550 seamen were idle; 900 were idle in New Westminster and Vancouver, making a total of 2.050. To be complete, of course, that figure has to include the 200 seamen working on part time.
I am sorry to have to discuss this resolution in the absence of the Minister of Trade and Commerce, because these subsidies are under his department. When I discussed the matter last year the then Minister of Trade and Commerce used the argument that the orientals engaged on board these steamers operating from the Pacific coast were British subjects. I should like to point out that after careful investigation it will be found that such a statement is very far from the fact. The orientals engaged on these steamers are engaged chiefly from Hongkong and from Canton, and I have before me some information as to the conditions existing in Hongkong, taken from the Encyclopedia Britan-nica. I am going to quote some of these conditions, because they are very pertinent to the matter under discussion in view of the statement made last year. Hongkong is a depot for an incessant flow of Chinese emigration and immigration, especially in connection with the Straits Settlements. In 1931 there were 100,869 emigrants and 283,890 immigrants. The total population of Hongkong in that same year was 840,473, and of these only some 19,000 were classified as non-Chinese.
Immigrants from the Canton delta account for 76 per cent of the total population of Hongkong. The colony is governed by a legislative council consisting of the governor, nine official members and six unofficial members. Eight of these members are nominated by the crown, of whom three are Chinese. The police force consists of approximately 2,110 men. The Chinese section has a system of watchmen, who are paid by the Chinese inhabitants.
One of my reasons for stating these facts, Mr. Speaker, is in order to bring before the house these conditions in an endeavour to prove that it is impossible for anyone to say definitely as to the nationality of these men, apart from the fact that they are hired through Hongkong. The total number of pupils in the public schools in 1931 was 68.500. Of these one-third were in schools in which English was the medium of instruction, while two-thirds were in the vernacular schools, in which Chinese is taught. The five schools for children of British parentage have an average attendance of only 423. Hongkong, being the principal source of distribution in the far east, with a huge floating population and a constant influx from the mainland not only by steam vessels but by local junks as well, it would appear to be no easy matter to keep a real check on the movements of the Chinese in that city. As to the number of those who have become British citizens there is no record available, and such information could be obtained only from the secretary of Chinese affairs. There is no system in vogue by which it would be possible to tell whether or not large numbers of Chinese in Hongkong have become naturalized, unless after a very careful inquiry and close check. I believe the city is divided into two sections, European and Chinese, with a wire fence between which is guarded by Chinese policemen. It is no trouble at all for the Chinese to pass through the European section into the Chinese section or vice-versa, and it is from this section that seamen are engaged on the steamships plying from Pacific ports.
As a matter of fact, Mr. Speaker, an investigation was conducted by the Seamen's Association of Vancouver, and they have evidence to prove that the majority of the Chinese on these ships operating from the Pacific coast claim to come from Canton. Here is how they are engaged. On each ship they have a number one boy who is paid S15 per month. Seamen are recruited at Canton by a shore agency which is operated by another agency in Hongkong. When engaged they are directed to the number one boy on the ship, who arranges as to their pay, and so on. This
